\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.10}{\ignorespaces Architectures and workflow investigated during the implementation of the routing model. Blue blocks represent inputs, green blocks denote processing stages, and orange blocks correspond to classification outputs.}}{81}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.11}{\ignorespaces High-level overview of the multi-stage chest disease classification pipeline.}}{84}{figure.caption.47}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.12}{\ignorespaces Chest X-ray images before and after preprocessing, highlighting improved contrast and reduced noise.}}{85}{figure.caption.48}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.13}{\ignorespaces The U-Net architecture, illustrating the encoder-decoder structure with skip connections that merge high-resolution features from the contracting path with upsampled features in the expansive path. This design is highly effective for precise medical image segmentation.}}{86}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.14}{\ignorespaces Application of the U-Net segmentation mask. The original preprocessed Figure~\ref {fig:proc-cxr} is processed to produce the final segmented lung Figure~\ref {fig:segmented-lung}, which serves as the input for the classification stage}}{87}{figure.caption.50}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.15}{\ignorespaces Grad-CAM visualizations highlighting regions contributing to model predictions.}}{87}{figure.caption.51}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.16}{\ignorespaces YOLOv8 architecture pipeline for automated localization and classification of pulmonary abnormalities.}}{89}{figure.caption.52}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.17}{\ignorespaces Qualitative evaluation showing tumor localization (confidence scores 0.72 and 0.60) on an axial CT slice.}}{91}{figure.caption.56}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.18}{\ignorespaces Qualitative evaluation showing tumor localization (confidence score 0.87) on an axial CT slice.}}{91}{figure.caption.56}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.19}{\ignorespaces Example of an interpretable YOLO detection result on a chest CT image. The model localizes a suspected lesion using a bounding box and reports a confidence score of 0.87. Such spatial predictions provide inherent interpretability by explicitly identifying the anatomical region associated with the model's decision, reducing the need for post-hoc visualization techniques.}}{92}{figure.caption.57}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.20}{\ignorespaces Detailed architectural diagram of the YOLOv8 object detection framework, illustrating the Backbone, Neck (FPN + PAN), and Detection Head stages for multi-scale prediction.}}{95}{figure.caption.63}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.21}{\ignorespaces Overall architecture of the proposed Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) model. The user query is transformed into vector embeddings, relevant medical knowledge is retrieved from the FAISS vector database, and the retrieved context is supplied to the Qwen 2.5 language model to generate a grounded and source-aware response.}}{103}{figure.caption.69}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.22}{\ignorespaces Query processing workflow of the proposed RAG system.}}{106}{figure.caption.71}%
